;; See docs/devtour/README.md for the format and how to add a block.
;;
;; Each step anchors on {:ns <namespace> :defn <symbol>} — NEVER a line number.
;; Variants: {:file "resources/packages/.../impls.clj" :defn <sym>} for package
;; impls outside src/, and {:defn ig/init-key :dispatch <val>} for a defmethod.
;; The generator bakes the anchored form's real source into index.html; CI
;; (`bb devtour-check`) fails if an anchor stops resolving or the HTML drifts.
{:title "Graphden — developer tour"
 :intro
 "This tour explains how Graphden stores, compiles, and runs [graph
           functions](#term/graph-function). It assumes basic Clojure and HTTP knowledge. A [graph
           function](#term/graph-function) describes a computation; an [argument slot](#term/slot)
           identifies one input, and a [binding](#term/binding) supplies that input. An [execution
           context](#term/context) selects storage, branch, caches, and execution policy. A [compiled
           registry](#term/compiled-registry) maps function IDs to callable Clojure
           [closures](#term/closure). Follow a linked term whenever you need its definition;
           definitions are outside the numbered steps and do not change reading progress.

           Read the blocks in [dependency order](#term/topological-order), starting with Executor.
           Boot is also a useful starting point if you want to understand process startup first. The
           Editor block reads JavaScript; the other blocks primarily read Clojure. The user-facing
           editor lessons are separate from this developer tour.

           Every step displays actual source from a named form. See-also links open related steps, and
           Back returns along your navigation path. Definition links open a separate reference view;
           in Emacs, Return to the tour closes its help window.

           The same source generates this standalone page, the Emacs data, and the Org reading files.
           See [setup](../../docs/devtour/README.md). For build, tests, isolated worktrees, and the
           merge queue, read [CLAUDE.md](../../CLAUDE.md) and [worktree
           workflow](../../dev/wtq/AGENT.md)."

 :glossary [
  {:id "graph-function" :title "Graph function"
   :say "A stored definition of a computation, identified by a function ID. It can declare arguments
           or specialize parent functions by [inheritance](#term/inheritance) and
           [bindings](#term/binding). A function ID identifies the definition; its name is a label
           within a namespace."}
  {:id "base-function" :title "Base function"
   :say "A [graph function](#term/graph-function) with a registered host-language implementation.
           Composed functions obtain the implementation through their parents and supply arguments
           through graph [bindings](#term/binding). A base function is distinct from the base branch
           used in version resolution."}
  {:id "context" :title "Execution context"
   :say "The runtime value carrying storage, implementations, caches, and execution policy. It
           determines the data and branch visible to a call. A branch-specific context may resolve the
           same function ID differently from another context."}
  {:id "compiled-registry" :title "Compiled registry"
   :say "A map from [graph function](#term/graph-function) IDs to callable Clojure
           [closures](#term/closure) produced by compilation. It is cached in an [execution
           context](#term/context) and updated or invalidated after relevant graph changes. This is
           different from the type registry or package inventory."}
  {:id "closure" :title "Closure"
   :say "A callable function that retains values from the environment in which it was created. In
           this tour, graph reachability is called the [reachable graph](#term/reachable-graph); it
           must not be confused with a callable closure."}
  {:id "slot" :title "Argument slot"
   :say "The identity of a graph argument, with a declaring function, type, and public name.
           Renaming may create a view linked through source-slot-id. Slot identity is different from
           its displayed name and from a [concurrency permit](#term/concurrency-permit)."}
  {:id "binding" :title "Binding"
   :say "Stored information specifying a graph argument: a literal value, a reference, sequence
           items, or caller-supplied input. Effective bindings can be inherited. This differs from
           Clojure [dynamic bindings](#term/dynamic-binding), which temporarily supply values to
           dynamic vars during execution."}
  {:id "dynamic-binding" :title "Clojure dynamic binding"
   :say "A Clojure var declared `^:dynamic` can receive a temporary, thread-local value through
           `binding`. Nested calls see that value until the scope exits and restores the earlier
           value. Graphden uses this for execution policy, tracing, and cancellation. Asynchronous
           work must convey these bindings when it needs the same context. This does not create a
           stored graph [binding](#term/binding) or a lexical [closure capture](#term/capture)."}
  {:id "free-argument" :title "Free argument"
   :say "An input that the current function has not supplied internally and exposes to its caller.
           Identity comes from the underlying [slot](#term/slot) even when its public name changes.
           Some free arguments reached through a callback are supplied by [capture](#term/capture)."}
  {:id "inheritance" :title "Function inheritance"
   :say "A [graph function](#term/graph-function) names parent functions and inherits their argument
           structure and [bindings](#term/binding), subject to the graph rules. Multiple inheritance
           combines several parents. This parent relation uses parent-ids; branch ancestry uses
           base-branch-id and is separate."}
  {:id "thunk" :title "Deferred computation / thunk"
   :say "A callable wrapper representing work to perform when an argument is read. A delay also
           defers work and caches its result. Deferral allows a conditional to leave the unselected
           branch unevaluated; it is not a promise that every deferred result is cached forever."}
  {:id "hof" :title "Higher-order function / HOF"
   :say "A function that accepts or returns another callable function, such as map accepting a
           per-element callback. Graphden uses function types and parameter metadata to distinguish
           arguments passed on each callback invocation from [captured input](#term/capture)."}
  {:id "capture" :title "Closure capture"
   :say "Supplying a callback [free argument](#term/free-argument) from the enclosing caller instead
           of treating it as a per-invocation parameter. For example, a callback can receive each item
           from map while retaining a threshold supplied by its caller."}
  {:id "effect" :title "Effect"
   :say "An operation involving external state or the environment, such as network access or a
           database write. Declared effect information supports checks; runtime enforcement checks
           actual recorded effects against the currently allowed set. A pure function has no such
           effects."}
  {:id "invalidation" :title "Cache invalidation"
   :say "Marking cached data as no longer current, removing it, or updating affected entries after a
           change. An epoch is a counter used to detect whether cached data was built before relevant
           changes. Notifications can accelerate this process but may be missed."}
  {:id "memoization" :title "Memoization"
   :say "Remembering a function result under a key derived from its inputs so a repeated request can
           reuse it. Correctness depends on the key including relevant inputs and on the lifetime of
           the cache; per-execution caching differs from process-wide caching."}
  {:id "topological-order" :title "Dependency order"
   :say "An ordering in which each dependency is processed before the function or package that needs
           it. A topological ordering exists for an acyclic directed graph. This is not necessarily
           the visual order of nodes on the canvas."}
  {:id "reachable-graph" :title "Reachable graph / BFS / DFS"
   :say "The nodes reached by repeatedly following the relevant reference edges from a starting
           node. Breadth-first search (BFS) visits one distance level at a time; depth-first search
           (DFS) follows a path before returning. Which edges count depends on the traversal contract."}
  {:id "protocol" :title "Protocol and storage decorator"
   :say "A Clojure protocol declares methods implemented by different backends. A storage decorator
           implements the same protocol while wrapping another storage object, adding behavior such as
           branch resolution without changing caller method names."}
  {:id "advisory-lock" :title "Advisory lock"
   :say "A PostgreSQL lock selected explicitly by application code using a shared key. It
           coordinates cooperating callers, for example around a uniqueness check and write.
           Transaction-scoped locks release at transaction end; session locks belong to the
           connection."}
  {:id "cte" :title "Recursive SQL / CTE"
   :say "A common table expression (CTE) gives a named intermediate query result using WITH. WITH
           RECURSIVE allows subsequent rows to refer to earlier results; Graphden uses it to traverse
           references in the database."}
  {:id "branch-view" :title "Resolved branch view"
   :say "The effective entity rows visible on a branch after considering own versions, branch
           ancestry, merged versions, and [deletion markers](#term/tombstone). Identity rows identify
           entities; version rows carry branch-dependent content. A comparison resolves each branch
           independently."}
  {:id "tombstone" :title "Deletion marker / tombstone"
   :say "A version recording that an entity is deleted in a branch view. It can hide inherited
           content without physically deleting a shared identity. Physical deletion has stricter
           cross-branch safety checks."}
  {:id "branch-local" :title "Branch-local function"
   :say "A function excluded from normal merge propagation when it or an ancestor has branch-local?
           true. The effective flag is inherited by OR: false on a descendant does not undo a true
           ancestor. This mechanism is intended for branch-specific configuration."}
  {:id "subtype" :title "Subtyping and refinement"
   :say "Actual is a subtype of expected when every value allowed by actual is also allowed by
           expected. A refinement restricts a base type with a condition, such as an integer greater
           than zero. Nullability concerns whether null is allowed, not whether a field must be
           present."}
  {:id "unification" :title "Type variables and unification"
   :say "A type variable stands for a type not yet fixed, such as the element type a in a list.
           Unification chooses compatible substitutions for variables. The occurs check rejects
           substitutions that make a variable contain itself. Directional assignment still needs a
           separate check."}
  {:id "rich-types" :title "Rich-types registry"
   :say "A registry of computed argument types, return type, [effects](#term/effect), and related
           metadata for each function. It is scoped to the active branch. The compiler and UI read it,
           but it is separate from the [compiled registry](#term/compiled-registry) of executable
           [closures](#term/closure)."}
  {:id "secret" :title "Secret marking and redaction"
   :say "A secret type marker tracks whether a value may reveal sensitive input. Propagation keeps
           that marker through computations. Redaction removes sensitive data from persisted or
           displayed results; it does not mean the computation never used the original value."}
  {:id "hiccup" :title "Hiccup and DOM"
   :say "Hiccup represents markup as Clojure data: vectors contain a tag, optional attribute map,
           and children. Rendering produces HTML. DOM is the browser representation of the page;
           constructing Hiccup does not itself install browser event handlers."}
  {:id "ring" :title "Ring, Reitit, and middleware"
   :say "Ring represents HTTP requests and responses as Clojure maps; a handler maps a request to a
           response. Middleware wraps a handler to add processing. Reitit matches request paths and
           methods to route handlers."}
  {:id "htmx" :title "HTMX and partial fragments"
   :say "HTMX uses HTML attributes to request and replace page fragments. In this editor, partials
           are server-rendered HTML inserted into an existing host element. Local action dispatch and
           stale-response protection are still browser-runtime responsibilities."}
  {:id "reconciliation" :title "Reconciliation"
   :say "Comparing desired configuration with actual state and applying the differences. A periodic
           pass can recover from missed notifications or process failure. Service reconciliation
           starts and stops instances; package synchronization reconciles authored definitions with
           stored rows."}
  {:id "concurrency-permit" :title "Execution concurrency permit"
   :say "An admission token used to limit concurrent execution requests, such as runs admitted for
           an organisation or process. A run acquires it before submission and releases it on
           completion or failed submission. It is not an argument [slot](#term/slot), and it is
           separate from the advisory-lock permits coordinating [service
           instances](#term/cardinality)."}
  {:id "cardinality" :title "Service cardinality"
   :say "The service placement rule: one instance across participating executors (singleton), up to
           N coordinated instances (pool), or an instance per executor process (per-pod). An
           [advisory-lock](#term/advisory-lock) permit enforces coordinated counts; it is unrelated to
           a function [argument slot](#term/slot)."}
  {:id "fleet" :title "Execution fleet and cell"
   :say "A fleet is a group of executor processes. A cell identifies the workload for an
           organisation and entry function. Placement maps that cell to the executor serving it; a
           heartbeat records that an instance is still active."}
  {:id "sse" :title "Server-sent events / SSE"
   :say "A long-lived HTTP stream that lets a server deliver events to a client. Graphden uses it
           for [invalidation](#term/invalidation) and UI updates. PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY is a
           separate database notification mechanism that can feed these updates."}
  {:id "integrant" :title "Integrant and addon"
   :say "Integrant initializes and halts application components from configuration data and
           dependency references. An addon supplies extra configuration and component implementations
           through existing interfaces. It need not be a fork of the core application."}
  {:id "auth" :title "Authentication, authorization, and capability"
   :say "Authentication identifies the caller. Authorization decides whether that caller may perform
           an operation. A capability names one permission checked by policy. An authenticated
           principal is the identity information returned by an authentication provider."}
  {:id "ssrf" :title "SSRF and address revalidation"
   :say "Server-side request forgery makes a server contact a destination the attacker could not
           reach directly. Address validation must consider DNS changes and redirects: checking a name
           earlier is insufficient if the actual connection later uses a different address."}
  {:id "checkpoint" :title "CRaC checkpoint / restore"
   :say "An optional JVM facility for saving a prepared process state and restoring it later. Open
           sockets and other external resources need coordinated shutdown and reopening around the
           snapshot. Availability depends on the runtime and deployment setup."}
  {:id "edn" :title "EDN, keywords, and classpath"
   :say "EDN is a data format used for Graphden definitions. A keyword such as :name is distinct
           from the string \"name\"; namespaced keywords can identify references. The classpath is the
           collection of code and resource locations visible to the JVM."}
  {:id "mcp" :title "MCP"
   :say "Model Context Protocol: a communication protocol through which tools
           such as coding assistants call server-provided operations. Graphden exposes optional graph
           operations through its MCP package; those writes still use the normal branch and graph
           validation paths."}
  {:id "semver" :title "Semantic version and installation pin"
   :say "Semantic versions describe major, minor, and patch releases and support dependency
           constraints. An installation pin selects a particular package version. A pin is not the
           [branch-local](#term/branch-local) flag that excludes function changes from merging."}]

 :blocks
 [{:id :executor
   :title "Executor — running graph functions"
   :status :toured
   :summary "From stored definitions to callable code and results."
   :paths ["src/graphden/executor/"]
   :after []
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.executor.interface :defn execute
     :say "`execute` accepts an [execution context](#term/context), a [graph
           function](#term/graph-function) ID (`fn-id`), and named [free
           arguments](#term/free-argument). The context determines which storage and branch the call
           sees. This public entry validates the argument map and delegates execution to
           `compile-runtime`."
     :see [[:executor "create-context"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn execute
     :say "Runs a previously compiled [graph function](#term/graph-function). It first checks
           cancellation and any configured execution-permission guard, then binds the [allowed
           effects](#term/effect) for the call. The [compiled registry](#term/compiled-registry) maps
           function IDs to [closures](#term/closure): callable Clojure functions retaining their
           prepared dependencies. `execute` looks up the [closure](#term/closure), translates named
           input into slot-ID and name keys, and calls `(closure translated ctx)`."
     :see [[:executor "registry"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.context :defn create-context
     :say "Creates an [execution context](#term/context): storage,
           [base-function](#term/base-function) implementations, [compiled
           registry](#term/compiled-registry), graph caches, and permission/[effect](#term/effect)
           checks. The [compiled registry](#term/compiled-registry) is filled when needed. Two
           contexts can resolve the same `fn-id` differently, for example when they select different
           branches."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.context :defn fill-graph-cache!
     :say "Loads the graph cache while protecting concurrent writes. Before reading storage, the
           loader records the [invalidation](#term/invalidation) [epoch](#term/invalidation), a
           counter incremented when data changes. It installs its result under a lock only if that
           counter is unchanged; otherwise an older read could overwrite the graph installed by a
           newer write."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn registry
     :say "Returns the [compiled registry](#term/compiled-registry), a map from function IDs to
           Clojure [closures](#term/closure). An existing registry can be served while a background
           rebuild refreshes it. If no registry exists yet, compilation runs under the
           [invalidation](#term/invalidation) lock; a second check prevents concurrent callers from
           compiling the same context twice. Later writes can invalidate or update this registry."
     :see [[:executor "rebuild!"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn rebuild!
     :say "Reads stored graph rows, builds lookup indexes, and compiles functions. It then fills the
           graph and dependency caches and replaces the [compiled registry](#term/compiled-registry).
           The following steps explain how persisted function definitions become callable Clojure
           [closures](#term/closure)."
     :see [[:executor "build-lookups"] [:executor "compile-all"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.composition.core :defn sync-fns-to-storage!
     :say "Synchronizes authored function definitions into function, [slot](#term/slot), and
           [binding](#term/binding) rows. This writes the persisted graph later read by `rebuild!`.
           Package declarations and runtime compilation therefore share the same stored graph model."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.lookups :defn build-lookups
     :say "Builds lookup maps for functions, [slots](#term/slot), [bindings](#term/binding), and list
           items, including `:fn-map`, `:slot-map`, and `:bindings-by-fn`. Compiler helpers use these
           indexes instead of repeatedly searching the raw rows. The inheritance-chain cache records
           the ancestors used to resolve inherited values."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.lookups :defn root-fn
     :say "Follows `parent-ids` to find the root [base function](#term/base-function), whose
           implementation computes the result. A composed [graph function](#term/graph-function)
           supplies inherited or overridden arguments to that implementation. These function parents
           are distinct from the version branches discussed later."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.bindings :defn collect-bindings
     :say "Classifies the root [slots](#term/slot) through `classify-slot`: `:value`, `:free`, `:ref`,
           `:seq`, `:resolved-value`, or `:fn-ref`. These describe how to obtain each argument: read a
           value, receive caller input, evaluate a reference, assemble a sequence, use a resolved
           value, or pass function identity. Results are [memoized](#term/memoization) in the lookup
           data for compilation."
     :see [[:executor "arg-builder"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn compile-all
     :say "Compiles functions in [dependency order](#term/topological-order): a referenced function
           must be available before a caller uses it. The process cache is keyed by graph structure,
           [base-function](#term/base-function) implementations, and [rich type
           information](#term/rich-types). Types affect whether a referenced function is passed as a
           callable or evaluated, so different type information must not share the same compiled
           result."
     :see [[:executor "compile-fn"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn compile-fn
     :say "Builds one runtime [closure](#term/closure) `(fn [fa ctx])`. It prepares argument builders,
           [bindings](#term/binding) to inputs outside the root's slots, and name aliases at compile time.
           At execution time
           the [closure](#term/closure) assembles the argument map from caller input and those
           builders, then calls `(impl args ctx)`."
     :see [[:executor "arg-builder"] [:executor "resolve-impl"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn arg-builder
     :say "Builds a [deferred computation](#term/thunk) for one argument. Values are constants; [free
           arguments](#term/free-argument) read caller input by [slot](#term/slot) ID with a name
           fallback; references use [thunks](#term/thunk) or delays; sequences compute their elements
           lazily. A base implementation therefore evaluates only the arguments it reads, which
           permits short-circuiting conditionals."
     :see [[:executor "resolve-arg"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn resolve-impl
     :say "Finds the root [base function](#term/base-function)'s Clojure implementation in
           `lookups[:base-fns]` by name. A missing registration raises `:compile/missing-impl`. This
           lookup connects a stored graph definition to executable host code."
     :see [[:executor "defbase"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.defbase :defn defbase
     :say "Defines a base implementation whose argument symbols read from the runtime argument map
           through `resolve-arg`. The macro rewrites the declaration into a Clojure function receiving
           arguments and context, while preserving lazy access to referenced arguments."
     :see [[:executor "resolve-arg"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.runtime :defn resolve-arg
     :say "Reads one argument: evaluates a [thunk](#term/thunk) when needed, dereferences a delay or
           another value implementing `IDeref` (the Clojure interface for `deref`/`@`), or returns an
           ordinary value. The base implementation triggers referenced computations only by reading
           the corresponding argument. Compare with `arg-builder`, which prepared that deferred value."
     :see [[:executor "arg-builder"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn call-with-cache
     :say "Caches referenced-function results during one top-level execution. The key includes the
           referenced function ID and the relevant [free arguments](#term/free-argument); a sentinel
           distinguishes a cached nil from a missing entry. Reusing a result avoids evaluating the
           same dependency repeatedly within that call."}
    {:file "resources/packages/core/hof/impls.clj" :defn map-fn
     :say "`map-fn` is a [higher-order function](#term/hof): `func` receives a callable prepared by
           `hof-wrap`. `doall` realizes the result sequence while the Clojure [dynamic
           bindings](#term/dynamic-binding) for [effect](#term/effect) checks, tracing, and
           cancellation are still active. Deferring realization beyond this scope would run the
           callbacks after those [dynamic bindings](#term/dynamic-binding) had ended."
     :see [[:executor "arg-builder"]]}
    {:file "resources/packages/core/recursion/impls.clj" :defn fix-fn
     :say "Implements explicit recursion. Direct cycles through `ref-fn-id` are rejected during
           writes; `:fix` instead passes `{:input … :self …}` to its step function, which may call
           `:self`. `*max-recursion-depth*` bounds recursion depth. [Recursion
           design](../../docs/RECURSION.md) explains the alternatives considered."}
    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.renames :defn hof-lambda-params
     :say "Determines which [free arguments](#term/free-argument) are callback parameters and which
           are captured from the caller. An explicit `:lambda-params` takes precedence; an empty
           vector [captures](#term/capture) every [free argument](#term/free-argument). Otherwise an
           unambiguous structural function type supplies the parameter names. See [closure
           capture](../../docs/CLOSURE_CAPTURE.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.renames :defn build-ref-renames
     :say "Builds the name translation at a reference call. If a caller exposes an argument under a
           new name but the referenced function still uses its original name, the translation supplies
           the value under the referenced function's name. See the [inherited rename
           design](../../docs/adr/ADR-inherited-rename-surface.md)."
     :see [[:crud "public-free-entries"]]}
    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.renames :defn compute-rename-aliases
     :say "Computes aliases for inherited rename chains that reach outside the root's own
           [slot](#term/slot) set. For example, an internal reader of `:branch-row` can receive input
           exposed publicly as `:item`. The aliases are prepared at compile time and copied into the
           input map at runtime; the docstring contains a worked example."}]}

  {:id :storage
   :title "Storage — schemas and graph reads"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Protocols, rows, traversal, and change notifications."
   :paths ["src/graphden/storage/" "src/graphden/schema/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.storage.protocol.core :defn Storage
     :say "Defines the storage lifecycle [protocol](#term/protocol): initialize a backend with a data
           schema, then close its resources when finished. Row operations and reachable-graph queries
           are separate [protocols](#term/protocol) shown next."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.core :defn StorageCRUD
     :say "Defines row operations such as `read-entity`, `query-entities`, and `create-entity`. These
           [protocol](#term/protocol) methods let graph code read and write entities without depending
           on a concrete database implementation."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.core :defn ExecutionGraph
     :say "Defines `resolve-execution-graph`: given a root function ID, return the [reachable
           graph](#term/reachable-graph) and its indexes. Generic and PostgreSQL implementations
           follow below. This is a separate API from the executor's full-registry rebuild, which
           currently queries the graph entity tables through `StorageCRUD`."
     :see [[:executor "rebuild!"]]}
    {:ns graphden.schema.protocol.protocol :defn DataSchema
     :say "Defines how consumers inspect entity declarations, fields, and field types such as
           references, enums, and unions. Storage uses this schema data to understand rows before
           reading or writing them."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.malli.core :defn MalliDataSchema
     :say "Implements `DataSchema` with immutable maps of entities, enums, entity UUIDs, and retired
           fields. [Protocol](#term/protocol) methods read those maps. `create-builder` constructs the
           initial schema; each subsystem's `extend-builder` adds its declarations."
     :see [[:boot "postgres"]]}
    {:ns graphden.schema.graph.schema :defn extend-builder
     :say "Declares the graph entities, their fields, reference edges, and uniqueness constraints. The
           schema builder turns these declarations into database tables and indexes. Retired-field
           declarations prevent obsolete columns such as `binding.rename-to` from being recreated."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.fields.types :defn valid-type?
     :say "Checks actual field values against declared storage types. Known types have predicates;
           unknown types are currently accepted. Row-write validation calls this function, so its
           permissiveness is part of the current storage contract."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn ExecutionGraphResult
     :say "Defines the resolved execution-graph result: entity collections together with indexes by
           function and [binding](#term/binding). Both generic and PostgreSQL resolvers return this
           same structure."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn ->execution-graph
     :say "Assembles an execution-graph result and its lookup indexes from loaded entity collections.
           Both resolver implementations share this construction step."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn process-fn-node
     :say "Collects the outgoing references of one function: parents, type references, and functions
           referenced by [bindings](#term/binding) or list items. The reachable-graph traversal uses
           these edges to decide which functions to visit next."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn resolve-execution-graph-bfs
     :say "Traverses the [reachable graph](#term/reachable-graph) [breadth
           first](#term/reachable-graph) ([BFS](#term/reachable-graph)): visit the current functions,
           collect their references, then visit previously unseen targets. PostgreSQL's [recursive
           query](#term/cte) implements the same edge traversal in the database."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.generic-graph :defn resolve-execution-graph
     :say "Implements reachable-graph resolution using generic row loaders and
           [breadth-first](#term/reachable-graph) traversal. It works with any `StorageCRUD` backend,
           but the number of database calls grows with the visited graph. The PostgreSQL-specific
           resolver below batches those reads."
     :see [[:storage "StorageCRUD"]]}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.core :defn PostgresStorage
     :say "Implements the storage [protocols](#term/protocol) for PostgreSQL, mostly by delegating
           each method to a helper. Its `ExecutionGraph` implementation selects the optimized
           PostgreSQL resolver rather than the generic traversal. Read the [protocol](#term/protocol)
           headings to distinguish lifecycle, row operations, constraints, and graph resolution."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.migration :defn do-initialize
     :say "Compares the declared schema with stored metadata and applies the required database changes
           in a transaction. A transaction-scoped [advisory lock](#term/advisory-lock) is acquired
           before reading metadata, so concurrent process startups do not plan migrations against the
           same outdated state. See [operations](../../docs/OPERATIONS.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.graph :defn reachable-fns-sql
     :say "Builds a [recursive SQL](#term/cte) [common table expression](#term/cte) ([CTE](#term/cte))
           that follows outgoing references from the starting function and returns reachable function
           IDs. Compare its edge list with `process-fn-node` to check that SQL and Clojure traversals
           include the same dependencies."
     :see [[:storage "process-fn-node"]]}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.graph :defn resolve-execution-graph
     :say "Runs the [recursive query](#term/cte) for reachable function IDs, checks the graph-size
           limit, then loads related entity rows in batches and assembles the result. Batching keeps
           database round trips independent of the number of visited functions."
     :see [[:storage "->execution-graph"]]}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.notify :defn make-emitter
     :say "Creates the write-notification sender. After a write, it sends `pg_notify` through the
           pooled DataSource so other processes can refresh their state promptly. Notification failure
           does not undo the stored write; [reconciliation](#term/reconciliation) remains responsible
           for recovering current state."
     :see [[:crud "notify-after-write!"]]}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.notify :defn create-listener
     :say "Receives `graphden_events` on a dedicated PostgreSQL connection because `LISTEN` belongs to
           a connection session. A background thread polls and reconnects with increasing delays after
           failures. Callback failures are isolated; branch [invalidation](#term/invalidation),
           service [reconciliation](#term/reconciliation), and browser update notifications subscribe
           here."
     :see [[:versioning "dispatch*"] [:services "reconcile-once!"]]}]}

  {:id :versioning
   :title "Branches — versions and merges"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Branch-specific reads, conflict detection, and cache refresh."
   :paths ["src/graphden/versioning/" "src/graphden/system/branch_router.clj"
           "src/graphden/system/branch_router/"]
   :after [:storage]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.versioning.storage.core :defn wrap-with-versioning
     :say "Wraps ordinary storage with versioning, ensures a `main` branch exists, and returns storage
           bound to that branch. Callers retain the same storage [protocol](#term/protocol) while
           reads and writes gain branch-specific behavior."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.core :defn VersionedStorage
     :say "This [storage decorator](#term/protocol) forwards non-versioned entities unchanged. For
           versioned entities it resolves reads in the current branch and appends [version
           rows](#term/branch-view) on writes. Follow the delegated functions to inspect each
           operation separately."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.core :defn create-entity-versioned!
     :say "Creates an [identity row](#term/branch-view) and a [version row](#term/branch-view),
           accounting for an existing deterministic ID. Function names and list positions must be
           unique in the [resolved branch view](#term/branch-view), so their checks and writes use an
           [advisory lock](#term/advisory-lock). A global table constraint would incorrectly reject
           legitimate differences between branches."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.tx :defn in-transaction
     :say "Finds the underlying database pool and rebuilds the [storage-decorator](#term/protocol)
           chain around a transaction connection. Each write still passes through its existing
           [decorators](#term/protocol). Transaction-scoped [advisory locks](#term/advisory-lock) used
           by uniqueness checks remain held until the transaction ends."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn entity-config
     :say "Lists the versioned entities: `:fn`, `:fn-slot`, `:binding`, `:binding-list-item`, and
           `:resource-override`. For each entity it distinguishes identity fields in the base table
           from `version-data-fields` stored in [version rows](#term/branch-view). Resolution and
           writes use this configuration."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.versioned.schema :defn derive-version-fields
     :say "Derives version-table fields from the base entity declarations. Identity-level fields
           remain outside the version table; other fields require stable mirror IDs. Deriving both
           schema and write configuration from the same source prevents newly added fields from being
           silently omitted."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn resolve-version
     :say "Resolves one entity as visible on a branch by reading and combining its version candidates.
           The selected version is returned, or nil when a [deletion marker](#term/tombstone) wins."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn collect-branch-chain
     :say "Follows `:base-branch-id` from a branch to its parent and root, caching the resulting
           chain. Version resolution and [cache invalidation](#term/invalidation) use it to determine
           which branches can contribute visible data. This is branch ancestry, separate from
           [function inheritance](#term/inheritance) through `:parent-ids`."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn resolve-version-from-cache
     :say "Resolves cached version candidates along a branch's ancestry. At each level it considers
           locally written and merged versions using their effective timestamps. Single-entity and
           whole-graph resolution share this algorithm."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.branch-local :defn effective-branch-local?
     :say "Returns true when a function or one of its ancestors has `:branch-local? true`. Merge
           resolution uses that effective flag to exclude branch-specific configuration. Setting false
           on a descendant does not cancel a true flag inherited from an ancestor."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.uniqueness :defn check-list-item-position-collisions!
     :say "Checks list-item position uniqueness in the [resolved branch view](#term/branch-view). Both
           ordinary writes and merges use this check. A database constraint across all branches would
           prevent legitimate divergence; the write path therefore serializes the branch-specific
           check and write with an [advisory lock](#term/advisory-lock). Function-name uniqueness uses
           the same approach."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.uniqueness :defn check-resource-override-path-collision!
     :say "Checks that a branch view has at most one resource override per asset path.
           `:resource-override` stores replacement JS/CSS content; `:read-resource-overridable`
           chooses the effective content when serving it. Saving changes the asset version hash so
           browsers request the updated file."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.purge :defn purgeable-identity-ids
     :say "Decides which [identity rows](#term/branch-view) may be physically deleted. Ordinary user
           deletion writes a [tombstone](#term/tombstone); synchronization and rollback sometimes need
           physical deletion. An identity may be removed only when no other branch keeps a version and
           no child identity still references it, preventing dangling identities without deleting
           another branch's data."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.identity-repair :defn repoint-refs!
     :say "Repairs references after moving a packaged function between namespaces, which changes its
           deterministic ID. Updates references in both identity and [version
           rows](#term/branch-view); `purge-fn-subgraph!` removes the abandoned data when safe. The
           live-reference check counts current versions on live branches rather than every historical
           reference."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.merge :defn diff-branches
     :say "Compares the resolved contents of two branches, considering entities visible from either
           side. This read-only comparison reports their differences without deciding how a merge
           should resolve conflicts."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.diff-view :defn diff-branches-view
     :say "Groups branch differences by owning function and adds per-field before/after values. It
           also resolves [slot](#term/slot) names and function references in batches. The editor's
           comparison and review interfaces consume this display model; the older API retains its flat
           row format."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.merge :defn detect-conflicts
     :say "Finds entities changed to different content on both branches after their fork point,
           excluding [branch-local functions](#term/branch-local). Effective timestamps include
           changes that arrived through earlier merges, not only rows originally written on that
           branch. These conflicts need resolution before merging."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.merge :defn merge-branch!
     :say "Validates conflicts and approval requirements, then writes a `branch-merge` record and any
           conflict-resolution overrides in one transaction. Resolution makes source versions visible
           through this record without copying all source rows. The result also reports rows excluded
           as [branch-local](#term/branch-local)."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.merge.core :defn merge-transitively!
     :say "Merges missing ancestor branches before the requested source branch, starting with the
           oldest required ancestor. Each merge passes the same policy checks as a direct merge. The
           result lists preliminary merges in `:merged-first`. See [versioning
           limits](../../docs/VERSIONING.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.merge.core :defn validate-approval-policy!
     :say "Enforces a target branch's review policy. Required approvals must be distinct, current,
           bound to this merge target, and from allowed reviewers when `:approver-ids` restricts them.
           Missing valid approvals produce `:branch/approval-required` rather than allowing the merge."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.merge.core :defn branch-content-stamp
     :say "Computes a content stamp from the count and latest timestamp of the branch's own [version
           rows](#term/branch-view). An approval stores this stamp. Later edits append rows and change
           the stamp, so the approval no longer counts for the changed content."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.graph-epoch :defn bump!
     :say "Increments the graph [epoch](#term/invalidation) before a graph-changing write. The branch
           router checks this counter when retrieving a context, so it can detect missed invalidations
           even when a request was interrupted or a notification was lost. Immediate cache updates and
           notifications reduce refresh latency."}
    {:ns graphden.system.branch-router :defn build-branch-ctx
     :say "Creates a branch-specific [execution context](#term/context) with storage bound to that
           branch, independent graph and compiled-registry caches, and a copy of the branch's [rich
           type information](#term/rich-types). The router binds those types while compiling and
           handling requests, preventing another branch's types from determining this branch's
           behavior."
     :see [[:executor "create-context"]]}
    {:ns graphden.system.branch-router :defn dispatch*
     :say "Resolves a request's branch reference from a header or query parameter and invokes that
           branch's handler. Unknown branches return 400. The public wrapper handles `/livez` before
           branch compilation so the liveness probe does not depend on the [compiled
           registry](#term/compiled-registry)."}
    {:ns graphden.system.branch-router.epoch :defn validate-graph-epoch!
     :say "Checks graph [epochs](#term/invalidation) since the context's last recorded position and
           distinguishes applied, notified, in-progress, and missed changes. Missed changes trigger a
           background rebuild for the base context and eviction of other cached branch contexts, which
           rebuild on demand."}]}

  {:id :types
   :title "Types — validation and inference"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Type expressions, compatibility, inference, and secret markings."
   :paths ["src/graphden/types/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.types.core.shapes :defn primitives
     :say "Lists primitive value types and the special types `:any` and `:never`. `:any` accepts every
           type; `:never` has no values. Compound types such as lists, records, and functions build on
           this vocabulary."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core.shapes :defn child-types
     :say "Returns the nested types inside each compound type: records, lists, maps, tuples,
           refinements, secrets, unions, and functions. This traversal shows the data representation
           used by the rest of the type checker."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn well-formed?
     :say "Validates the syntax of type expressions before later checker stages use them. Cases verify
           the required shape of function arguments, [effects](#term/effect), refinements, secret
           wrappers, and other supported types."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn make-union
     :say "Normalizes a union type: flattens nested unions, removes duplicates and `:never`, and
           removes alternatives already covered by another member. A union means that a value may have
           any one of its member types."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn desugar-variant
     :say "Converts a variant declaration into a union of records whose tag field has a fixed value.
           This lets the checker use existing record, union, and [refinement](#term/subtype) rules
           instead of a separate runtime representation for variants."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn subtype?
     :say "Tests whether every value allowed by `actual` is also allowed by `expected`. Its ordered
           cases handle special types, unions, primitives, refinements, structural types, and [secret
           markers](#term/secret). For unions, every actual alternative must fit, while an expected
           union needs at least one matching alternative. Read the case for the two shapes being
           compared."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn constraint-implies?
     :say "Checks whether one [refinement condition](#term/subtype) implies another, including numeric
           bounds and set membership. [Subtype](#term/subtype) checking uses it to compare types such
           as positive and non-negative integers."
     :see [[:types "subtype?"]]}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn unify
     :say "[Unification](#term/unification) finds substitutions for [type
           variables](#term/unification) that make two type expressions compatible. The [occurs
           check](#term/unification) prevents a variable from being defined recursively through
           itself. Structural cases recurse into function, collection, and record types; the result is
           a substitution map or `::fail`. Directional assignability is checked separately."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn strip-null
     :say "Removes null from a top-level union; a type containing only null becomes `:never`. Secret
           wrappers remain intact. Control-flow checks and `:coalesce` use this shared narrowing
           operation."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core.shapes :defn taint-with-secret-if-tainted
     :say "Marks a return type as secret when an input contains a secret and the function's rule
           propagates that marker. The `[:secret …]` wrapper carries information needed for subsequent
           type checks and result [redaction](#term/secret); it does not itself store the secret
           value."
     :see [[:types "subtype?"]]}
    {:ns graphden.types.check.literals :defn classify-literal
     :say "Infers a structural type from a literal Clojure value, including collections, records, and
           primitive values. [Binding](#term/binding) checks use this inferred type when checking
           concrete supplied values."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn check-binding!
     :say "Checks one [binding](#term/binding)'s actual type against the [slot](#term/slot)'s expected
           type. It combines literal classification, [subtype](#term/subtype) checks, and
           type-variable [unification](#term/unification). Since [unification](#term/unification)
           chooses substitutions rather than proving assignment direction, `gradual/fits?` checks the
           resulting direction afterward."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check.gradual :defn fits?
     :say "Checks assignment direction after [unification](#term/unification). A wider type must not
           pass into a narrower [slot](#term/slot), such as `:numeric` into `:int` or a
           [nullable](#term/subtype) type into a non-nullable type. `erase` handles the checker's
           explicit gradual-typing allowances before `subtype?` compares the remaining types."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn signature-return
     :say "Infers a return type from a [polymorphic signature](#term/unification) when there is no
           custom `:return-type-rule`. It unifies declared argument types with actual types and
           substitutes the resulting variable values into the declared return type. Custom rules
           remain necessary when inference depends on literal values or registry data."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn rule-return
     :say "Chooses return-type inference for the root [base function](#term/base-function): custom
           rule first, declared signature next, then static return type. If the root propagates
           [secret markings](#term/secret) and an input is secret, the result is wrapped accordingly."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn check-fn-def!
     :say "Checks one function definition against its parents and inherited [slots](#term/slot). It
           validates argument changes and [effects](#term/effect), checks [bindings](#term/binding),
           computes the return type, and records detailed type information for later dependent
           functions."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn check-all-defs!
     :say "Checks definitions in [dependency order](#term/topological-order), registering each result
           before dependent definitions need it. This performs the full graph type sweep using the
           same single-definition checker."}
    {:ns graphden.executor.registry.core :defn record-rich-types!
     :say "Stores each function's computed argument types, return type, and [effects](#term/effect) in
           the [rich-types registry](#term/rich-types). Compiler decisions, runtime checks, [secret
           redaction](#term/secret), and editor labels read this data. Writes target the active
           branch's registry, so checking one branch does not overwrite another branch's types."
     :see [[:executor "compile-all"] [:web "api-rich-types"]]}
    {:ns graphden.executor.registry.core :defn rich-type-of-id
     :say "Looks up [rich type information](#term/rich-types) by function ID. Names may repeat across
           namespaces and are not a unique identity. The remaining name index supports [base
           functions](#term/base-function) and older call sites; new consumers should use IDs. See the
           [identity model](../../docs/adr/ADR-identity-model.md)."}]}

  {:id :crud
   :title "Graph API — reads, writes, and execution"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Request validation, stored edits, execution results, and views."
   :paths ["src/graphden/crud/"]
   :after [:storage :types]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.crud.request :defn extract-entity-params
     :say "Extracts entity type and ID from an HTTP mutation request. It prefers parsed
           [Reitit](#term/ring) path parameters and falls back to parsing the request URI."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.request :defn read-json-body
     :say "Normalizes a [Ring](#term/ring) request body supplied as text, an input stream, or an
           already parsed value. JSON object keys become Clojure [keywords](#term/edn) for the write
           handlers."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.branches :defn resolve-branch-ref
     :say "Resolves the requested branch by UUID or name before writing. The resulting branch
           determines which versioned-storage view receives the change."
     :see [[:versioning "wrap-with-versioning"]]}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities :defn create-entity
     :say "Creates one row through the generic write API. It checks the caller's
           [capability](#term/auth), validates the proposed change with `write-rej`, writes through
           `sp/create-entity`, then invalidates caches and sends notifications."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.validation :defn write-rej
     :say "Checks whether a proposed mutation must be rejected before storage is changed. Checks
           include reference cycles, [multiple-inheritance](#term/inheritance) collisions, terminal
           functions, closed lists, and secret paths. Both create and update operations use this
           validation."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities.record-type :defn apply-create-record-type-body
     :say "Creates the function row, [slot](#term/slot) rows, and function-slot links for a record
           type. Each successful write is appended to a journal. The journal supports compensating
           cleanup if a later write fails; this sequence is not a database transaction."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities.record-type :defn apply-create-rollback
     :say "On failure, traverses the creation journal backward and attempts to delete each created
           row. This is best-effort compensation, not transactional atomicity: cleanup can itself
           fail, in which case the code logs the need for manual cleanup."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities :defn apply-create-core
     :say "Creates a row, checks the resulting function's types, and applies rename-slot updates.
           Ordinary type errors leave the edit stored and return `:type-warnings`.
           [Secret-flow](#term/secret) violations instead delete the new row and return an error,
           keeping the security rejection independent of the diagnostics store. See
           [secrets](../../docs/SECRETS.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.type-check :defn type-check-fn-after-mutation!
     :say "Reconstructs and checks the changed function, updating branch-specific diagnostics.
           `:reject-secret?` marks secret-related failures for the caller to reject instead of storing
           them as ordinary warnings. The following `type-check-fn-and-dependents!` also checks direct
           callers using the compiler's reverse dependency index."
     :see [[:types "check-fn-def!"]]}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities.invalidation :defn notify-after-write!
     :say "Invalidates affected caches after a stored change and emits a PostgreSQL notification for
           other processes. The notification carries update information so their compiled functions
           can refresh."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution.lookup :defn resolve-fn
     :say "Resolves the function ID or name from an execution request into the stored function row.
           This lookup begins request execution and result persistence."}
    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.surface :defn public-free-entries
     :say "Computes public [free arguments](#term/free-argument) for execution forms, service
           validation, and graph placeholders. It combines renamed public arguments with [captured
           arguments](#term/capture) found inside [higher-order functions](#term/hof), removes inputs
           supplied by enclosing scopes, and deduplicates by [slot](#term/slot) identity. These
           consumers therefore share one definition of required input."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution :defn apply-execute
     :say "Coordinates `/api/execute`: resolves the function, version, and [free
           arguments](#term/free-argument); acquires a [concurrency permit](#term/concurrency-permit);
           submits execution to a future; then handles pending, successful, or failed results. This is
           a request-management layer around the executor from the first block."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution.persist :defn create-pending-with-args!
     :say "Writes a pending execution row together with its argument and argument-item rows
           atomically. This records which inputs belong to the execution before its final outcome is
           available."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution.persist :defn write-finished!
     :say "Stores a completed future's result, error, or cancellation on the execution row.
           Secret-derived values are [redacted](#term/secret) before persistence so polling sees the
           final safe result."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.value-repr :defn render-repr
     :say "Chooses a [graph function](#term/graph-function) that renders a result value as
           [Hiccup](#term/hiccup), using the declared or inferred type and falling back to literal
           classification. It runs with `:allowed-effects #{}` and sanitizes the output before
           insertion into the editor [DOM](#term/hiccup). See [typed result
           representations](../../docs/EXECUTION.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.value-form :defn resolve-form
     :say "Converts an effective [slot](#term/slot) type into a pure form descriptor for a record,
           list, union, or leaf. It resolves aliases and variants on the server and bounds recursion
           depth, so the browser need not reimplement the type grammar."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.value-form :defn build-form
     :say "Renders a form descriptor as [Hiccup](#term/hiccup): records become field groups, unions
           get a choice of variant, and leaf types use the registered form functions. `GET
           /partials/value-form` returns this [server-rendered fragment](#term/htmx), which
           [HTMX](#term/htmx) inserts into the inspector. See [partials](../../docs/PARTIALS.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.debug-capture :defn run-captured!
     :say "Runs the next matching HTTP request with tracing when a one-shot debugger interception is
           armed for its organisation and branch. The interception expires after a limited time.
           Sanitized request and response data are stored as an execution so the existing call-tree
           interface can display the handler run. See [debugging](../../docs/EXECUTION.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.secrets :defn apply-create-secret-body
     :say "Creates graph rows containing a secret path, then stores the value and metadata in OpenBao.
           The value never enters the graph rows. A journal supports compensating rollback if a later
           operation fails. Request parsing and validation are composed in `app/secrets/fns.edn`; this
           function performs the coordinated writes. See [secrets](../../docs/SECRETS.md)."
     :see [[:types "taint-with-secret-if-tainted"]]}
    {:ns graphden.crud.test-runs :defn run-tests!
     :say "Runs functions from `tests` namespaces through the ordinary execution API with empty
           arguments; a run passes if it does not throw. Status belongs to the current function
           version, so an edit makes earlier results stale. Each completed test notifies the editor.
           Tests owned by shipped packages are excluded from the default user test run. See
           [tests](../../docs/TESTS.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.test-autorun :defn schedule-affected!
     :say "Finds tests affected by a write through reverse dependencies. It selects tests with no
           [effects](#term/effect), combines closely spaced requests into one delayed run, and runs
           them with conveyed [dynamic bindings](#term/dynamic-binding). `:allowed-effects #{}` enforces the no-effects
           restriction during execution as well."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities.list :defn graph-list-env
     :say "Builds a shared lazy read environment over the graph cache. Separate projections produce
           namespace trees, function lists, searches, subtrees, or a full graph listing.
           Server-assigned roles distinguish types from functions. This is the shared data source
           behind editor list refreshes."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities.list :defn view-members
     :say "Evaluates graph filters for the Explorer and saved graph views. Filter dimensions are
           combined with AND; `:uses` follows reverse dependencies by function ID, and `:views`
           intersects other views with a cycle check. Ad-hoc filtering and the saved `:explorer-view`
           function use this same evaluator."}]}

  {:id :packages
   :title "Packages — loading and distribution"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Declarations, implementations, synchronization, import, and lint."
   :paths ["src/graphden/packages/" "resources/packages/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-packages
     :say "Loads configured packages in [dependency order](#term/topological-order) and combines their
           module definitions into `:base-fn-defs`, `:fn-defs`, and `:namespaces`. Boot uses these
           results to populate the stored graph and implementation registry."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn resolve-dependencies
     :say "Orders packages by their declared dependencies using [depth-first
           search](#term/reachable-graph) ([DFS](#term/reachable-graph)). Transitive dependencies are
           included, so a package's required primitives load before functions that use them."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn validate-dep-constraints!
     :say "Checks each package's declared [semantic-version constraints](#term/semver) against the
           available [classpath](#term/edn) packages. A mismatch stops boot with
           `:packages/version-conflict`, before missing or incompatible definitions can cause later
           loading errors."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-single-package
     :say "Reads one package's `package.edn` and loads its listed modules. The result combines the
           [base-function](#term/base-function) definitions and composed function definitions from
           those modules."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn process-module
     :say "Loads a module's `fns.edn` and `impls.clj`, then separates [base
           functions](#term/base-function) requiring implementations from composed functions and type
           declarations. This is where the two module files are combined."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn base-fn?
     :say "Classifies an entry as a [base function](#term/base-function) when it has no parent
           declarations and is not a type row. [Base functions](#term/base-function) require a host
           implementation; composed definitions inherit their implementation through parents."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-module-fns
     :say "Reads a module's [EDN](#term/edn) definitions while retaining source file and line
           information. Later validation and type errors use those locations to identify the authored
           definition."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.records.parse :defn parse-module
     :say "Converts module definitions into stored rows in two passes. First it assigns deterministic
           IDs; then it parses definitions using the module and previously known definitions to
           resolve inherited [slot](#term/slot) ownership before writing any rows."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.records.parse :defn parse-composed
     :say "Parses a composed function's parents and arguments. A bound argument creates a
           [binding](#term/binding) to an inherited [slot](#term/slot); a type-only argument
           declaration can add an own [slot](#term/slot). `resolve-slot-owner` determines which
           ancestor owns the targeted [slot](#term/slot)."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.records.slot-resolution :defn resolve-slot-owner
     :say "Finds the function that declared an argument's [slot](#term/slot), searching
           [inheritance](#term/inheritance) and referenced-function paths and using types to
           disambiguate names. The resulting [slot](#term/slot) ID is stored in the
           [binding](#term/binding) so later compilation preserves argument identity."
     :see [[:executor "build-lookups"]]}
    {:ns graphden.packages.records.slot-resolution :defn build-rename-slot-records
     :say "Represents `{Y {:as :X}}` by creating an own rename [slot](#term/slot) linked to the
           original [slot](#term/slot) through `source-slot-id`. Parser, executor, and editor use this
           link to keep public names separate from underlying [slot](#term/slot) identity."
     :see [[:executor "build-ref-renames"]]}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-impls-via-eval
     :say "Evaluates `impls.clj` forms in a namespace and reads its `impls` map from function names to
           Clojure implementations. During coverage tests, an already instrumented namespace can be
           reused so those calls remain visible to the coverage tool."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn fn-def->base-fn-def
     :say "Combines an `fns.edn` declaration with its Clojure implementation. Argument types, return
           type, [effects](#term/effect), and implementation become one
           [base-function](#term/base-function) registry entry."
     :see [[:executor "resolve-impl"]]}
    {:file "resources/packages/core/arithmetic/impls.clj" :defn add
     :say "A concrete arithmetic [base function](#term/base-function): `(defbase add [nums]
           (safe-arith + nums :add))`. The loader collects this implementation and pairs it with the
           `:add` declaration from `fns.edn`."
     :see [[:executor "defbase"]]}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn sync-namespaces!
     :say "Creates the stored namespace hierarchy, such as `core` and `core.arithmetic`, and returns a
           map from namespace paths to IDs. Function synchronization uses these IDs to place each
           definition in its namespace."
     :see [[:executor "sync-fns-to-storage!"]]}

    {:ns graphden.packages.sync :defn sync-fn-entities-from-packages!
     :say "Synchronizes packaged definitions into storage, repairs moved identities, removes unused
           retired definitions, and checks types in [dependency order](#term/topological-order).
           Packages on disk describe the desired state. Test bootstraps may skip the full type sweep;
           production boot and `bb type-sweep` run it explicitly."
     :see [[:types "check-all-defs!"] [:versioning "repoint-refs!"]]}

    ;; --- Export & distribution: the loader's inverse -------------------
    {:ns graphden.packages.export :defn records->fn-defs
     :say "Converts stored graph records back into function definitions for export and distribution.
           Parsing the exported definitions must reconstruct the same graph: `parse(export(parse(x)))
           == parse(x)`. The corpus test verifies this property on actual packages."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.export :defn ref-kw
     :say "Chooses an unqualified [keyword](#term/edn) for a unique reference name and a
           namespace-qualified [keyword](#term/edn) when names repeat. Some version-qualified
           namespaces produce valid in-memory [keywords](#term/edn) whose printed form is not valid
           [EDN](#term/edn); the next step handles that text boundary."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.records.wire :defn encode-unreadable-kws
     :say "Before writing [EDN](#term/edn) text, replaces otherwise unreadable [keywords](#term/edn)
           with tagged literals such as `#graphden/ref`. Registered readers restore the original
           [keywords](#term/edn) during import. In-memory and JSONB paths use their own
           value-preserving codecs."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.export :defn external-deps
     :say "Finds references leaving an exported namespace subtree and records them as dependencies.
           References into version-qualified package namespaces also produce package dependencies. The
           surrounding `:export-namespace` [graph function](#term/graph-function) assembles the final
           bundle."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.export :defn strip-secret-paths
     :say "Removes vault paths from shared bundles by default and leaves the corresponding secret
           arguments free. The `:secrets` manifest tells the installer which values need to be defined
           locally; publishing a function therefore does not publish its secret locations."}
    {:file "resources/packages/registry/registry/impls.clj" :defn publish-package-apply
     :say "Stores a published bundle as an immutable `:package-version` row identified by version and
           content hash. Installation materializes definitions in a version-qualified namespace and
           records a [pin](#term/semver). Updating or rolling back changes references to the selected
           package version."}
    {:file "resources/packages/registry/registry/impls.clj" :defn upsert-pin!
     :say "Creates or updates an [installation pin](#term/semver). Only a new installation increments
           the global package statistic, using an atomic database upsert; moving an existing
           [pin](#term/semver) to another version does not. See
           [marketplace](../../docs/MARKETPLACE.md)."}
    {:file "resources/packages/registry/marketplace/impls.clj" :defn moderate-package-version!
     :say "Applies a platform administrator's moderation decision. The [capability](#term/auth) check
           is inside the write operation; the read uses platform storage so organisation scoping does
           not hide another publisher's row. After the change, `tc/notify!` lets the
           [addon](#term/integrant) notify the publisher. See [marketplace
           moderation](../../docs/MARKETPLACE.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loaded :defn install!
     :say "Records the packages actually loaded by this executor, including version, origin, and
           whether they include Clojure implementations. The editor displays this inventory. Packages
           containing executable Clojure code enter through the trusted build, not through this
           inventory API."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.starter-catalogue :defn seed!
     :say "Seeds starter themes, keymaps, and examples from `starter-catalogue.edn` at boot. It uses
           the same package-version representation as publishing and skips existing name/version
           pairs. Changing an immutable listing requires a new version. See [starter
           catalogue](../../docs/MARKETPLACE.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.sync :defn adopt-bundle-identities!
     :say "During bundle import, aligns editor-created same-name identities with deterministic IDs and
           redirects their references. The surrounding graph pipeline chooses the target branch,
           excludes package-owned definitions, synchronizes the bundle, and optionally removes missing
           definitions. This identity-level repair is specific to import, not ordinary
           [MCP](#term/mcp) upsert."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.sync :defn reconcile-bundle-scope!
     :say "Implements `?prune=true` within the bundle's namespaces. Definitions absent from the
           snapshot receive branch-specific [tombstones](#term/tombstone), including inherited
           definitions. It does not physically purge shared identities; referenced removals are
           retained and reported."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.git-format :defn bundle->files
     :say "Writes a bundle as namespace module files plus a manifest with deterministic output for Git
           comparison. Nested argument-map order is preserved because parsing derives
           [slot](#term/slot) positions from it. The CLI uses these files for HTTP export, import,
           push, and pull."}
    {:ns graphden.lint.core :defn lint
     :say "Checks function definitions for duplicate implementations, unused private definitions,
           redundant inherited [bindings](#term/binding), siblings that could share a parent, and
           excessively deep [inheritance](#term/inheritance). Both CI and the editor use this same
           linter over function-definition data. See [graph lint](../../docs/GRAPH_LINT.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.lint.graph :defn lint-branch
     :say "Converts the current organisation and branch's graph snapshot into function definitions and
           runs the graph linter. Results are cached while the snapshot object is unchanged. The lint
           filter and inspector section share this result; graph-stored suppressions remove
           acknowledged findings."}]}

  {:id :boot
   :title "Boot — starting the system"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Configuration, component dependencies, startup, and shutdown."
   :paths ["src/graphden/executor_runtime/" "src/graphden/system/"]
   :after [:storage :packages]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.executor-runtime.core :defn -main
     :say "The JVM entry point starts the [Integrant](#term/integrant) component system, installs a
           shutdown hook, optionally opens nREPL, then keeps the process alive. The HTTP server starts
           separately through service [reconciliation](#term/reconciliation); `-main` does not itself
           handle requests."
     :see [[:boot "boot!"] [:services "reconcile-once!"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor-runtime.core :defn boot!
     :say "Starts the system and, on failure, halts components that already initialized before exiting
           with an error. [Integrant](#term/integrant) does not automatically halt a partially
           initialized system, so this cleanup prevents surviving threads from keeping a failed
           startup alive."}

    {:ns graphden.system.config :defn read-config
     :say "Reads profile configuration with Aero, then merges [addon](#term/integrant) fragments
           listed in `GRAPHDEN_ADDON_CONFIGS`. [Addons](#term/integrant) can replace or add
           [Integrant](#term/integrant) component keys without changing core configuration files. With
           no [addons](#term/integrant), the core configuration is used unchanged. See [addon
           configuration](../../docs/TENANCY_SEAM.md)."
     :see [[:platform "platform-tier?"]]}

    {:ns graphden.system.interface :defn start!
     :say "Passes the loaded configuration to `ig/init`, which initializes components in [dependency
           order](#term/topological-order). Requiring the `init.*` namespaces registers their
           `ig/init-key` implementations before this call. The next steps show the
           main components."}

    {:ns graphden.system.init.storage :defn ig/init-key :dispatch :db/postgres
     :say "Opens the PostgreSQL connection pool and initializes the declared schema. An optional
           `:datasource-wrap` lets the tenancy [addon](#term/integrant) attach organisation context to
           each connection for row-level security. The versioned-storage component subsequently wraps
           this storage."
     :see [[:versioning "wrap-with-versioning"]]}

    {:ns graphden.system.init.packages :defn ig/init-key :dispatch :app/packages
     :say "Loads core, web, app, [addon](#term/integrant) packages, and external packages from the
           operator manifest. The following [base-function](#term/base-function) and function-entity
           components register implementations and synchronize graph definitions into storage."
     :see [[:packages "load-packages"] [:packages "sync-fn-entities-from-packages!"]]}

    {:ns graphden.system.init.exec :defn ig/init-key :dispatch :exec/context
     :say "Builds the base [execution context](#term/context), including [authentication](#term/auth),
           request scoping, execution checks, vault access, notifications, and [fleet](#term/fleet)
           forwarding. Branch-specific contexts are derived later. `:compile-storage` may provide a
           privileged structural graph view; it must not be confused with organisation-scoped
           application data reads."
     :see [[:executor "create-context"]]}

    {:ns graphden.system.init.exec :defn ig/init-key :dispatch :exec/compiled-registry
     :say "Compiles the graph before serving the first request so the base context starts with a
           populated [compiled registry](#term/compiled-registry). Subsequent graph changes can still
           invalidate or update it; this startup compilation does not make the registry permanent."
     :see [[:executor "rebuild!"] [:executor "registry"]]}

    {:ns graphden.system.route-collection :defn dispatch-first
     :say "Tries optional registered [Ring](#term/ring) routers in order. A router returns nil to let
           the next router handle the request; when none handles it, the application router is used.
           Packages and [addons](#term/integrant) can register routes without changing the core
           handler chain."}
    {:ns graphden.system.api-url-drift :defn check-router!
     :say "Compares literal `/api/*` and `/partials/*` paths in editor JavaScript with the actual
           router during boot. A missing route prevents startup. This catches changes to those
           statically discoverable paths; dynamically constructed URLs still need their own tests."}
    {:ns graphden.cli :defn -main
     :say "The CLI is a second entry point using the same application artifact. It transfers and
           compares graph branches through authenticated HTTP APIs using export, import, diff, push,
           and pull. It does not require direct database access."}
    {:ns graphden.crac :defn register-checkpoint-hooks!
     :say "Registers optional [CRaC](#term/checkpoint) [checkpoint/restore](#term/checkpoint) hooks.
           Before a [checkpoint](#term/checkpoint), schedulers pause and open connections and
           listeners close; restore resumes them. These hooks do nothing on an ordinary JVM. See
           [fleet checkpoint design](../../docs/FLEET_RFC.md) and `development/crac/`."}]}

  {:id :web
   :title "Web — HTTP handlers and rendering"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Requests, routing, markup, and graph-backed APIs."
   :paths ["resources/packages/web/" "resources/packages/mcp/"
           "src/graphden/crud/types_api.clj"]
   :after [:packages :boot]
   :steps
   [{:file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :defn http-server
     :say "Starts an http-kit listener with the composed [Ring](#term/ring) handler on a configured
           port and records network/process [effects](#term/effect). This [base
           function](#term/base-function) manages the listener; surrounding request and response
           behavior is composed in `web/http/fns.edn`."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :defn realize-request-body
     :say "Reads an incoming request body completely so graph handlers receive reusable data rather
           than a stream that can be consumed only once."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn ring-handler-fn
     :say "Combines a compiled [Reitit](#term/ring) router with the default handler into the
           [Ring](#term/ring) function called for each HTTP request. The router selects the route; the
           default handler covers requests it cannot handle."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn ring-router-fn
     :say "Builds a [Reitit](#term/ring) [Ring](#term/ring) router from route data composed as graph
           values. The resulting routing table associates request paths with handlers."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn ring-create-default-handler-fn
     :say "Builds the fallback handler for requests with no matching route, method, or acceptable
           response. The corresponding 404, 405, and 406 responses are graph-composed values."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn middleware
     :say "Builds [middleware](#term/ring) from a [graph function](#term/graph-function) with [free
           arguments](#term/free-argument) `:request` and `:next-handler`. The returned
           [Reitit](#term/ring) `:wrap` function surrounds each route handler, allowing request
           processing shared across routes to be expressed in the graph."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/branch-router/impls.clj" :defn current-branch-router
     :say "Reads the installed branch router from its shared holder. It returns nil before boot
           installs the router; branch-dispatch [middleware](#term/ring) uses this value to reach
           branch-specific handlers."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/branch-router/impls.clj" :defn dispatch-to-branch
     :say "Delegates a [Ring](#term/ring) request to the handler for its selected branch. One
           listening server can therefore execute different branch-specific graphs. This is the
           package-level caller of the branch router explained in the versioning block."
     :see [[:versioning "dispatch*"]]}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/ring-adapter/impls.clj" :defn authenticate-request
     :say "Authenticates the request before page or action execution. This is part of the handler
           chain after routing, separate from later checks of what the authenticated caller may do."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/crud-write/impls.clj" :defn write-rej
     :say "Exposes pre-write validation as one [base function](#term/base-function) returning a
           rejection reason or nil. The surrounding CRUD routes compose parsing, validation, and
           application as [graph functions](#term/graph-function). Reference-cycle,
           [inheritance](#term/inheritance), override, and closed-list checks remain shared with the
           CRUD implementation."
     :see [[:crud "write-rej"] [:crud "apply-create-core"]]}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/crud-parse/impls.clj" :defn form-decode
     :say "Decodes form data and normalizes repeated-key values to the declared text-map result. It
           uses the form codec rather than a regular expression, avoiding regex input-length limits
           for large values such as edited assets."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :defn hiccup-element
     :say "Constructs a [Hiccup](#term/hiccup) element from tag, attributes, and children.
           Graph-composed pages use it to build markup, including `data-action` attributes handled by
           the browser runtime and [HTMX](#term/htmx) attributes requesting [server-rendered
           fragments](#term/htmx). This creates markup; browser interaction still requires the
           corresponding runtime."
     :see [[:editor "bindActionDispatch"] [:editor "loadPartial"]]}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :defn h-raw
     :say "Marks a string as raw [Hiccup](#term/hiccup) content so trusted script or style text is
           emitted without HTML escaping. This is used for bundled frontend assets. It must not be
           confused with sanitization of user-authored markup."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :defn render-hiccup
     :say "Serializes a [Hiccup](#term/hiccup) tree into HTML. Its type rule propagates [secret
           markings](#term/secret) so rendering a secret-derived value does not remove the information
           needed for [redaction](#term/secret)."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :defn pick-encoding-fn
     :say "Chooses response encoding from `Accept-Encoding`. The surrounding `:process-response`
           [graph function](#term/graph-function) decides whether compression is appropriate and
           prepares headers and body. This primitive contains the header-parsing logic shared by that
           composition."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.types-api :defn compatible-type-names
     :say "Finds named types that may narrow the expected type, resolving aliases and checking
           [subtyping](#term/subtype) on the server. The picker receives one rendered set of options
           instead of issuing a separate compatibility request for each type name."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.types-api :defn api-rich-types
     :say "Shapes the [rich-types registry](#term/rich-types) into the compact `/api/types` response
           used by the editor. Fields unnecessary for the bulk listing are omitted; specialized
           interfaces fetch or render the details they need separately."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.types-api :defn candidate-fit
     :say "Ranks already-admissible function candidates by how their parameters fit a callable
           [slot](#term/slot). `:exact` uses the supplied parameter count, `:captures` needs
           additional [captured input](#term/capture), and `:ignores` consumes fewer parameters.
           Ranking changes display order, not whether the type checker admits a candidate."}
    {:file "resources/packages/storage/branches/impls.clj" :defn sync-fn-defs-branch!
     :say "Synchronizes function definitions into a specified branch for [MCP](#term/mcp) and
           bundle-import callers. It uses the same synchronization checks as packaged definitions and
           invalidates the target branch's [compiled registry](#term/compiled-registry). The target
           may differ from the branch carrying the HTTP request."
     :see [[:executor "sync-fns-to-storage!"] [:boot "dispatch-first"]]}]}

  {:id :layout
   :title "Layout — graph structure and coordinates"
   :status :toured
   :summary "From graph rows and expansion state to nodes, edges, and a grid."
   :paths ["src/graphden/layout/"]
   :after [:web]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.layout.data :defn load-graph-entities-uncached
     :say "Loads the graph entity rows needed for layout as a snapshot. Versioned storage can supply a
           branch-cached batch; ordinary storage uses separate entity queries. Later layout stages
           transform this snapshot, while the context cache avoids repeated database reads."}
    {:ns graphden.layout.data :defn derive-fn-slot-views
     :say "Derives each function's visible argument rows from inherited [slots](#term/slot) and
           effective [bindings](#term/binding). Nearer [bindings](#term/binding) override more distant
           ones. `:source-id` retains the declaring ancestor's provenance for inherited
           [slots](#term/slot); it is not simply the current displayed function ID."}
    {:ns graphden.layout.graph :defn build-graph-elements
     :say "Walks the requested node expansions with cycle protection and collects graph elements.
           Post-processing adds optional-argument and [HOF](#term/hof) badges, adjusts
           captured-argument edges, removes duplicate overlays, and attaches display facts. The result
           supplies the client's node and edge lists."}
    {:ns graphden.layout.builder-helpers :defn emit-root-deep-frees!
     :say "Adds placeholders for required public inputs reached through bound references, including
           required [HOF](#term/hof) [captures](#term/capture) reported by
           `surface/public-free-entries`. Optional [captures](#term/capture) do not create required
           holes. The placeholder keeps the inner [slot](#term/slot) identity while targeting the root
           function so [binding](#term/binding) an input matches the Run form's public argument model."}
    {:ns graphden.layout.core :defn compute-layout-matrix
     :say "Assigns graph elements to grid columns and rows and reports layout validation results. The
           browser later measures actual card sizes and converts the grid into pixel coordinates. This
           separates graph ordering from DOM-dependent geometry."}
    {:ns graphden.layout.strip-facts :defn annotate
     :say "Attaches computed facts to function nodes: the inherited return-type alias, the [base
           function](#term/base-function) owning a return-type rule, and the ancestor responsible for
           [branch-local](#term/branch-local) behavior. The editor displays these server-computed
           facts instead of repeating the same traversals in JavaScript."
     :see [[:versioning "effective-branch-local?"]]}]}

  {:id :editor
   :title "Editor — browser behavior"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Loading, rendering, actions, forms, comparisons, and preferences."
   :paths ["resources/packages/app/editor/" "resources/packages/web/runtime/"]
   :after [:web :layout]
   :steps
   [{:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-main.js" :defn initGraph
     :say "Initializes the editor's plain JavaScript modules, concatenated in a fixed order. It first
           requests the namespace tree to determine [authentication](#term/auth) state, then starts
           secondary loads; function details load on demand. A 401 opens sign-in. See the [module
           map](../../docs/EDITOR_MODULES.md)."
     :see [[:editor "authFetch"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-data.js" :defn buildLookups
     :say "Indexes downloaded function, [slot](#term/slot), [binding](#term/binding), and list-item
           rows for client rendering. Maps such as `fnMap` and `bindingByFnSlot` replace repeated
           scans of the same arrays. Because writes rebuild these lookups, their cost affects editor
           refresh latency."
     :see [[:executor "build-lookups"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-graph-model.js" :defn graph
     :say "Stores nodes, edges, adjacency indexes, and animation state without a graph-rendering
           library. `window.graphView` exposes readiness and animation status used by browser tests.
           Rendering and measurement live in the surrounding editor modules."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-layout.js" :defn fetchBackendLayout
     :say "Requests grid layout from `/api/graph/layout` using the selected root and expansion state.
           The browser then measures cards and edge labels to calculate column widths and gaps. The
           server determines graph order; the browser fits that order to actual pixel sizes."
     :see [[:layout "build-graph-elements"] [:layout "compute-layout-matrix"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-render.js" :defn renderGraph
     :say "Coordinates one asynchronous render. A generation counter prevents an older render from
           replacing a newer one; a remembered anchor preserves the user's position while data loads.
           After applying layout changes, it measures [DOM](#term/hiccup) cards and adjusts positions
           to their actual sizes."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-overlay-fn.js" :defn createFnOverlay
     :say "Builds an absolutely positioned HTML function card; edges are separate SVG elements. The
           card contains inherited levels, a use-site heading for expanded references, and
           [slot](#term/slot) rows. These controls present the stored
           function/[slot](#term/slot)/[binding](#term/binding) model for editing."
     :see [[:layout "derive-fn-slot-views"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/web/runtime/graphden-runtime.js" :defn bindActionDispatch
     :say "Registers one delegated event listener on a host and resolves `data-action` through the
           action-handler registry. Newly inserted server fragments become interactive without
           individual listener registration. Registering the listener once prevents repeated openings
           of a reused popover from executing the same action multiple times."
     :see [[:web "hiccup-element"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/web/runtime/graphden-runtime.js" :defn loadPartial
     :say "Loads a [server-rendered HTML fragment](#term/htmx) into a host and attaches action
           dispatch. A request token ensures that a later request wins even if an earlier response
           arrives last. See the [partial-fragment contract](../../docs/PARTIALS.md)."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-row-actions.js" :defn loadRowActionsContent
     :say "Loads the row-action menu as a server fragment, caching HTML by request URL. Compatibility
           for adding a parent is recalculated against current client lookups rather than trusted from
           stale HTML. See [row actions](../../docs/EDITOR_ROW_ACTIONS.md)."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-fn-picker-rank.js" :defn pickerArrange
     :say "Orders function-picker candidates without accessing the [DOM](#term/hiccup) or network.
           Exact-name matches come first, followed by namespace groups and compatibility/fit ranking.
           Typed searches show all matches; browsing can fold groups. The server's `candidate-fit` in
           the Web block supplies fit metadata without changing type admissibility."}
    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-edit-modes.js" :defn enterArgValueEditMode
     :say "Asks `/api/value-form` for a widget appropriate to the [slot](#term/slot), then dispatches
           by `data-form-widget`. The client does not choose the widget from raw type tags. After
           saving, it reloads [bindings](#term/binding) and lookups because changing a value changes
           stored graph rows."
     :see [[:crud "render-repr"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-diff-mode.js" :defn gdEnterDiffMode
     :say "Loads grouped differences for a selected comparison branch. Explorer badges, canvas
           highlights, field changes, [effect](#term/effect) changes, and review comments use this
           data; comparison-only functions appear as additional display rows. Reads and edits still
           target the current branch. The comparison menu provides review and merge actions."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-prefs.js" :defn gdApplyThemePayload
     :say "Applies a validated theme payload as inline CSS custom properties on the body. The
           sanitizer permits known tokens and supported color values, rejecting injected declarations
           or URLs. A localStorage copy applies before first paint; saved server preferences reconcile
           afterward. See [themes](../../docs/MARKETPLACE.md)."}
    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-shortcuts.js" :defn gdApplyKeymap
     :say "Applies user key overrides by stable shortcut ID. The registry keeps defaults for reset and
           applies overrides when late shortcuts register too. Settings and shortcut help read the
           same registry so they display the effective keys."}
    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-auth.js" :defn authFetch
     :say "Sends authenticated editor requests and handles 401 by clearing the stored credential and
           opening sign-in. HTTP 4xx responses still resolve normally: callers must inspect the
           response status. A `try/catch` alone detects network exceptions, not rejected HTTP writes."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-tour.js" :defn _tourTick
     :say "Evaluates the current interactive lesson step against editor state. Lesson data comes from
           `app.tour/_tour-lessons`; this engine ensures required panels and targets are visible,
           checks the declared completion condition, and advances when it succeeds."}]}

  {:id :services
   :title "Services — instances and placement"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Reconciliation, service discovery, tracing, and executor placement."
   :paths ["src/graphden/services/" "src/graphden/fleet/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.schema.services.schema :defn extend-builder
     :say "Declares stored service configuration and the restart-policy and
           [cardinality](#term/cardinality) enums. A service row specifies a function to keep running;
           it is not versioned. [Reconciliation](#term/reconciliation) compares this desired
           configuration with actual running instances."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.services.schema :defn effective-pool-size
     :say "Converts [cardinality](#term/cardinality) into the number of
           [advisory-lock](#term/advisory-lock) permits: `:singleton` allows one instance, `:pool`
           allows N, and `:per-pod` runs independently on each process. These permits are concurrency
           controls, not graph [argument slots](#term/slot)."}
    {:ns graphden.services.reconciler :defn diff-desired
     :say "Purely compares enabled service rows with the in-process running set and returns
           `:to-start` and `:to-stop`. Separating this decision from starting or stopping services
           makes [reconciliation](#term/reconciliation) policy directly testable."}
    {:ns graphden.services.reconciler :defn reconcile-once!
     :say "Performs one [reconciliation](#term/reconciliation) pass: reads desired services, detects
           configuration changes, plans starts/stops, and attempts to acquire [execution
           permits](#term/cardinality). Both write notifications and periodic ticks invoke it, so
           later passes can recover after missed notifications or a crashed instance owner."}
    {:ns graphden.services.reconciler :defn start-service!
     :say "Starts the service function through the executor and retries startup failures according to
           the restart policy. When retries are exhausted it records `:start-failed-at`, preventing
           the [reconciler](#term/reconciliation) from continuously retrying without delay."}
    {:ns graphden.services.instances :defn endpoint-of
     :say "Reads endpoint metadata from the service handle and adds the starting executor's reachable
           host. The resulting host and port are recorded on the service-instance row, whose
           [heartbeat](#term/fleet) is refreshed during [reconciliation](#term/reconciliation). Other
           services resolve this row to find an active endpoint."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.generic-constraints :defn dependency-closure
     :say "Loads a referenced function's execution graph in one batch, then walks execution
           dependencies in memory for cycle detection. Identity-only references are excluded because
           naming a service does not execute it. Constraint-vector type names are resolved during
           traversal."}
    {:ns graphden.services.liveness :defn check-liveness!
     :say "Checks service handles for liveness and exit status. A stopped instance loses its stored
           row and [execution permit](#term/cardinality); restart policy then determines whether to
           restart any exit, only a failure, or neither. Active instances refresh their
           [heartbeats](#term/fleet)."}
    {:ns graphden.services.endpoint :defn resolve-endpoint
     :say "Resolves a service identity to an active endpoint on the caller's branch, preferring a live
           local instance. It may fall back to an addon-provided resolver; otherwise it reports
           `:service/not-running`. The `:fn-ref` argument passes identity without executing the named
           function."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution.trace :defn run-traced!
     :say "Connects traced service calls across HTTP using `X-Graphden-Trace`. The handler execution
           receives its own execution ID and stores its result linked to the caller's trace. Requests
           without that header do not create these linked trace rows."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.queue.schema :defn extend-builder
     :say "Declares a PostgreSQL-backed job queue with status, attempts, due time, and a visibility
           lock. Claiming uses `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`; notifications wake waiting consumers. The
           consumer loop is composed in graph definitions, while database operations remain small
           primitives."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.placement :defn placement-for
     :say "Finds the placement of an [execution cell](#term/fleet), identified by organisation and
           entry-function ID. Placement records identify the responsible executor; the highest
           [epoch](#term/invalidation) wins when records differ. Routing and relocation use this
           shared placement information."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.router :defn forward-or-nil
     :say "Forwards an internal request when it arrives at an executor that does not own the target
           [cell](#term/fleet). It looks up the current placement and sends HTTP to that executor,
           allowing callers to reach a [cell](#term/fleet) through another [fleet](#term/fleet)
           member."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.metrics :defn cell-weight
     :say "Combines a [cell](#term/fleet)'s dependency-graph size and current organisation load into a
           numeric weight. Placement algorithms use this estimate to compare [cells](#term/fleet); it
           is a scheduling estimate, not a measurement of every function's execution cost."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.packer :defn pack
     :say "Builds a placement by sorting [cells](#term/fleet) from heaviest to lightest and assigning
           each to the currently least-loaded executor. This greedy longest-processing-time-first
           strategy is an approximation for balancing load, not an exhaustive search for an optimal
           arrangement."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.rebalance :defn rebalance
     :say "Improves an existing placement one move at a time, moving a [cell](#term/fleet) from a
           busier executor when that reduces load spread sufficiently. `:min-improvement` prevents
           moves for negligible gains. This heuristic limits unnecessary moves but does not prove a
           globally minimum move count."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.control-loop :defn plan-tick
     :say "Plans one control tick without performing moves. Unassigned [cells](#term/fleet) receive
           initial placements immediately; rebalancing waits for imbalance to persist for
           `:sustain-ticks`. This delay avoids reacting to brief load spikes."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.control-loop :defn run-tick!
     :say "Reads current [cells](#term/fleet) and placements, calls `plan-tick`, then applies planned
           assignments and moves through the supplied `move-fn`. The leader-controlled component
           invokes this side-effecting control loop periodically."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.controller :defn move-cell!
     :say "Relocates one [cell](#term/fleet) in order: prepare and compile on the target, update
           placement with a newer [epoch](#term/invalidation), then evict the source copy. Preparing
           the target before rerouting keeps the old copy available during setup."}]}

  {:id :platform
   :title "Platform — extension interfaces and isolation"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Access policy, safe rendering, monitoring, and remote executors."
   :paths ["src/graphden/tenancy/" "src/graphden/auth/" "src/graphden/clients/"
           "src/graphden/monitoring/" "src/graphden/byo.clj"
           "src/graphden/system/sse.clj"]
   :after [:services]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.tenancy.context :defn platform-tier?
     :say "Tests whether the current request has platform-level context rather than an organisation
           context. The tenancy [addon](#term/integrant) binds `*current-org*`; core code reads this
           shared interface without importing private policy implementation."}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.context :defn notify!
     :say "Passes a domain event to an installed notification handler, such as a package-moderation
           decision. The [addon](#term/integrant) decides recipients and delivery. With no handler the
           event is ignored; delivery failure does not reverse the domain write."}
    {:ns graphden.auth.provider :defn single-token-provider
     :say "Provides core's single-token [authentication](#term/auth) behind the replaceable
           `:auth/provider` component. It compares credentials in constant time. An
           [addon](#term/integrant) can install session- or account-based [authentication](#term/auth)
           through the same provider interface."}
    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn cloud-forbidden-effects
     :say "Lists [effects](#term/effect) forbidden for sandboxed graph execution, including
           environment, I/O, network, process, and raw SQL access. Core's `record-effect!` enforces
           the currently allowed set; the [addon](#term/integrant) selects that set. `:raw-sql` is
           separate from controlled database operations."}
    {:ns graphden.clients.egress :defn check-target!
     :say "Validates outbound destinations to prevent [server-side request forgery](#term/ssrf)
           ([SSRF](#term/ssrf)). Resolution rejects prohibited local, private, and metadata addresses.
           DNS and socket checks repeat validation at connection time, reducing the risk that an
           address changes between validation and use."}
    {:ns graphden.web.hiccup-sanitize :defn sanitize-hiccup
     :say "Sanitizes graph-authored [Hiccup](#term/hiccup) before it enters the editor
           [DOM](#term/hiccup). It allows selected tags and attributes, limits depth and node count,
           and converts unsupported content to safe text where possible. Unrepresentable output
           returns nil."
     :see [[:crud "render-repr"]]}
    {:ns graphden.util.counters :defn count!
     :say "Increments process-wide event counters. Readers compare two snapshots to obtain changes
           over an interval without resetting shared counters, so independent monitoring consumers do
           not invalidate one another's measurements. See [monitoring](../../docs/MONITORING.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.monitoring.alerts :defn decide
     :say "Decides whether to raise, resolve, or leave an alert unchanged from interval counters and
           prior alert state. Scheduling and webhook delivery are separate from this pure decision.
           See [alerting](../../docs/MONITORING.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.system.sse :defn start-relay!
     :say "Relays branch and organisation [invalidation](#term/invalidation) events to subscribed
           customer-hosted executors through [server-sent events](#term/sse) ([SSE](#term/sse)). The
           remote executor can refresh its compiled graph after a change without periodically
           downloading the entire bundle."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.remote.core :defn RemoteStorage
     :say "Implements read-only `StorageCRUD` over graph rows fetched from the hub through HTTP.
           Writes are rejected. `refresh!` replaces the in-memory bundle, and the [SSE](#term/sse)
           relay described above triggers refresh after [invalidation](#term/invalidation) events."
     :see [[:storage "StorageCRUD"]]}
    {:ns graphden.byo :defn start-byo!
     :say "Assembles a customer-hosted executor using remote read-only graph storage, [SSE](#term/sse)
           [invalidation](#term/invalidation), and a local HTTP server. The same execution core runs
           the downloaded graph while the hub remains responsible for writes."}]}

  {:id :accounts
   :title "Accounts — identity and sessions"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Sign-in, account linking, tokens, and second-factor validation."
   :paths ["src/graphden/accounts/"]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.accounts.core :defn resolve-social-identity!
     :say "Resolves a social sign-in to an existing identity, links it to an account with the same
           verified email when allowed, or creates an account. The resulting stable account ID is used
           for [authorization](#term/auth) independently of the sign-in method."}
    {:ns graphden.accounts.core :defn mint-session!
     :say "Creates a stored session whose raw token is returned once; storage keeps its SHA-256 hash.
           Session kinds also distinguish verification links and pending second-factor steps, which
           must not authenticate as ordinary API sessions."}
    {:ns graphden.accounts.provider :defn accounts-provider
     :say "Authenticates an API bearer token or the HttpOnly session cookie and returns an account
           [principal](#term/auth). Organisation membership is resolved separately by the tenancy
           layer, so the accounts provider does not choose an organisation."}
    {:ns graphden.accounts.routes :defn make-router
     :say "Builds the account HTTP routes for sign-in, identity management, verification, and
           two-factor [authentication](#term/auth). It returns nil for unhandled paths so later
           routers can continue. Page presentation uses an injected graph renderer with a built-in
           fallback when graph rendering is unavailable."}
    {:ns graphden.accounts.totp :defn valid?
     :say "Validates time-based one-time passwords according to RFC 6238, allowing the adjacent time
           steps for clock skew and comparing codes in constant time. Reference vectors test the
           algorithm; account flows handle enrollment and confirmation separately."}]}

  {:id :constellation
   :title "Repositories — composition and release boundaries"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Core, addons, external packages, and revision consistency."
   :paths ["src/graphden/system/config.clj" "src/graphden/tenancy/context.clj"
           "src/graphden/packages/manifest.clj" "tools/open-core-seam.edn"
           "resources/executor-packages.edn" "external-packages/"]
   :after [:boot :packages :platform]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.system.config :defn addon-config-resources
     :say "Reads [addon](#term/integrant) configuration resources from `GRAPHDEN_ADDON_CONFIGS`.
           Without them, core runs independently. The public core must not depend on private
           [addons](#term/integrant): tenancy depends on core, and cloud assembly depends on both.
           This configuration boundary preserves that dependency direction."
     :see [[:boot "read-config"]]}
    {:ns graphden.system.config :defn merge-addon
     :say "Loads an [addon](#term/integrant)'s required namespaces, then merges its configuration over
           core configuration. Loading registers component implementations before initialization.
           Cloud and self-hosted deployments assemble the same core with different components rather
           than maintaining divergent copies. See [distribution](../../docs/PACKAGE_DISTRIBUTION.md)."
     :see [[:boot "context"] [:boot "dispatch-first"]]}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.context :defn install-org-cap-fn!
     :say "Installs the [addon](#term/integrant) predicate used to check organisation
           [capabilities](#term/auth). Core call sites depend on this interface rather than private
           [authorization](#term/auth) implementation. Platform [capability](#term/auth) checks have a
           corresponding installer. See the [tenancy extension interface](../../docs/TENANCY_SEAM.md)
           for default and [addon](#term/integrant) behavior."
     :see [[:platform "platform-tier?"]]}
    {:ns graphden.tenancy.context :defn tenancy-addon-active?
     :say "Reports whether the tenancy [addon](#term/integrant) is installed. Server-rendered text can
           use this fact when deciding whether organisation-specific wording applies. It is distinct
           from the current user's tier or a general feature flag."}
    {:file "test/graphden/open_core_seam_test.clj" :defn every-addon-facing-var-still-resolves-test
     :say "Verifies that public extension variables used by the private [addon](#term/integrant) still
           resolve. `tools/open-core-seam.edn` records that interface so removing an apparently unused
           core function fails core CI before breaking an external consumer."
     :see [[:crud "create-entity"]]}
    {:ns graphden.packages.manifest :defn read-manifest
     :say "Reads the operator manifest of external packages containing code and function definitions.
           The build includes these libraries in the application artifact, and boot loads their named
           packages. A name-only entry refers to a package already available on the
           [classpath](#term/edn)."
     :see [[:boot "packages"] [:packages "load-packages"]]}
    {:ns graphden.packages.manifest :defn extra-deps
     :say "Adds manifest dependencies to the build basis. [Pin](#term/semver) checks ensure the
           manifest and `deps.edn` select the same revisions; copy checks ensure local test copies
           match available sibling repositories. This keeps tested package content aligned with the
           shipped artifact."
     :see [[:packages "install!"]]}
    {:ns graphden.executor-runtime.core :defn -main
     :say "Cloud assembly starts the same core `-main` rather than supplying another executor. Release
           checks must keep the core revision used by cloud and by tenancy identical; otherwise the
           assembled deployment differs from the combination tested. Documentation, translations, and
           organisation templates live in separate repositories without becoming runtime dependencies."
     :see [[:boot "boot!"] [:boot "read-config"]]}]}
]}
